# Validation and verification rules

## Freshness thresholds

`getVerificationFreshness(lastVerified)` in `src/js/utils/helpers.js` returns one of four buckets based on days since `last_verified`:

| Bucket | Condition |
|---|---|
| `recent` | < 60 days |
| `fresh` | 60–90 days |
| `stale` | > 90 days |
| `missing` | absent or unparseable date |

`REVERIFICATION_THRESHOLD_DAYS = 90` in `validation-schema.js` is the canonical reverification interval; the 60-day `recent` cutoff is a display distinction only. Don't conflate them.

`missing` is not the same as `stale` — missing means unverifiable. A program with an unparseable date must land in `missing`, never be treated as fresh by default.

## Verification tiers

`verification_tier`: `website_confirmed` | `phone_confirmed` | `provider_attestation` | `unconfirmed`.

These record *how* a program was confirmed and are provenance-gated — set them only from an actual confirmation event, never derive them from the presence of other fields. Same applies to `last_intake_confirm_method` (`website | phone | provider_attestation`) and `last_successful_contact_method` (`phone | email | web_form | unknown`).

## Dates

All date fields are ISO 8601 (`YYYY-MM-DD` or full ISO). `validateISODate` rejects anything else, including strings that parse but don't round-trip — which is what catches invented values like month 13.

## Blocking vs. advisory

`scripts/validate-data.js` is a **blocking** gate: any error exits non-zero, failing `npm run build` and CI. It enforces unique `program_id`s, required fields, enum membership, and cross-file parity between `programs.json`, `programs.geocoded.json`, and the regional snapshots.

`src/js/data-validator.js` is the browser-side counterpart and is **advisory** — it logs warnings (e.g. probable duplicate programs) at runtime and must never block rendering. Console warnings on page load are expected and are not test failures; only errors are.

Both import the same schema module, so a rule added to `validation-schema.js` takes effect in both places at once.
